Title: "Example"
Id: "bf36807b-f215-4d73-b75e-114ee734274e"

Text: "`Example:` records a worked example without claiming a theorem. It may
have an optional command heading and its body may freely interleave declarations,
expressions, nested clause groups, and quoted Markdown prose.

```mlg-fragment
[\example.command]?
Example: <ExampleItem>*
Justification?: <HaveGroup>+
Aliases?: <AliasItemUnion>+
Writing?: \"<WritingAlias>\"+
References?: <ResourceHeader>+
Metadata?: <MetadataItemUnion>+
Id?: <OpenText>
```

An example item is a declaration statement, an expression, a nested clause
group, or quoted text. Items are checked in source order, so a declaration or
assumption established by one item is available to the items that follow it.
The `Example:` body is allowed to be empty.

```mlg-fragment
Example:
. X is \set
. \"Choose an element $x$ of $X$.\"
. x is X
. x = x
Id: \"ecf0e128-503a-4287-aaeb-e81de640ce26\"
```

Quoted items render as prose between the rendered mathematical clauses.
`Example:` supports `Justification:`, `Aliases:`, `Writing:`, `References:`, and
`Metadata:`, but it does not have a `Documented:` section."
Id: "7300e066-c54c-4812-98ef-06b06342182b"
